Original commit body from d321d6df513f2c087196c729514164ec3b46722a:

Owner ruled option (a) on INV-fatok.

A primitive declared under two boundaries with boundary_ruling:
call_site_undecidable is narrowed at match time by the stamped io_target_kind.
When the stamp ABSTAINS, the fallback row decides -- and which row that was
came from CATALOG_BOUNDARY_TYPES, a frozen module-level list identical for
every language. c.yaml's note "THIS ROW STAYS FIRST" described a property it
did not control: moving go.yaml's entire fs_read: block below ipc_recv:, with
the parsed content asserted byte-identical, did not move the loaded indices.

Which fallback is CONSERVATIVE is a property of the primitive. c's fgets was
fs_read-only, a false NEGATIVE (fs_read mints no taint source). go's
bufio.NewScanner is ipc_recv-only, a false POSITIVE. They need opposite
defaults and the mechanism could express one.

`abstains_to` names it per row. Absent, the registry order stands, so every
row shipped before this field is unchanged -- pinned against the shipped c and
haskell catalogues. Validated at load, because all three failure modes are
silent: a misspelled boundary, a boundary the primitive has no row for, and two
rows of one primitive naming different targets would each leave the catalogue
READING as though a fallback had been chosen while the registry order quietly
decided it, which is the defect this removes.

REORDERING THE LIST WAS NOT ENOUGH, and finding that out corrected the
diagnosis. resolve_mode_boundary_across_sites answers fs_read for an ABSENT
mode -- deliberately, since guessing fs_write from ignorance would re-create
the false positives the mode gate removes -- so select_by_mode selected
whatever fs_read row was present REGARDLESS of list position. Measured: with
go's bufio rows reordered ipc_recv-first, an unstamped call still classified
fs_read. That default is right for builtins.open, whose mode IS the
discriminator; it is a category error for a primitive with no mode argument at
all. A declared abstains_to now outranks the mode DEFAULT and only the default:
positive mode evidence still decides, and builtins.open is untouched.

INV-bagok rides on it. bufio.NewScanner/NewReader were ipc_recv unconditionally
on the note "When wrapping os.Stdin", true at 11 of 236 censused sites (4.7%).
The analyzer has stamped the answer correctly since WI-lipis and nothing could
use it, because _target_kind_discriminated_keys needs two or more read
boundaries before a stamp may choose and this primitive had one row.

A/B over six Go repositories, both arms cold, baseline asserted by grepping the
runtime files: 42 rows move ipc_recv -> fs_read, 0 chains added, 0 lost, every
move on a positive host_path stamp. Read back against source: jaeger
uiconv/reader.go:31 (os.OpenFile O_RDONLY -> bufio.NewReader), beads' JSONL
readers.

THE FIRST VERSION MOVED 51 AND FIVE WERE WRONG, which is why abstains_to exists
rather than a bare second row. Under the registry-order fallback alertmanager
cluster/tls_connection.go:136 (a TLS connection), caddy
httpredirectlistener.go:90 (a net.Conn from Accept) and three peers moved to
fs_read -- all five with io_target_kind ABSENT, moved by the default flipping
rather than by anything inferred, each losing a TRUE untrusted_input source.
None of the five moves now. Nine stopped moving in total, four of them
file-shaped rows that were also riding the default; the honest count is 42
evidence-backed retags.

No net_recv row, deliberately: bufio.NewScanner(conn) really is a network
receive, but no target kind names a network stream, so such a row could never
be SELECTED.

